{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"deployment","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deployment"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Workflow is designed for normal deployment targets. A workflow can span\nhours or months, but each host invocation should do bounded work and then return."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The host is not the durability boundary. Cloudflare Cron Triggers, Railway Cron\nJobs, Netlify Scheduled Functions, Vercel Cron, queues, and worker alarms only\nwake the runtime. The store decides what is due, who can claim it, and whether\nthe run has already advanced."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Workflow documentation and adapter work prioritizes partner\nenvironments first: Cloudflare, Railway, and Netlify. Vercel is still supported\nas an important compatibility target, but it should not be treated as the default\ndeployment path."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The deployment recipe is the same everywhere:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Put workflow state in a durable store."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start or resume a run from HTTP, queue, webhook, or application code."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use host cron, alarms, or scheduled functions to wake "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.sweep()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep every sweep under the host's timeout and memory budget."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"invocation-shapes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Invocation shapes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most apps need three entrypoints:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Entrypoint"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime call"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start a run"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.startRun"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"User action, API request, queue message"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resume a run"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.deliverSignal"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.deliverApproval"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Webhook, payment event, admin approval"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wake background work"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.sweep"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cron, scheduled function, worker alarm"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each entrypoint claims a run, drives it to completion or the next pause, and\nreturns."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"bounded-sweeps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bounded sweeps"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Always budget background sweeps:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await runtime.sweep({\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxDurationMs"},{"type":"text","value":" below the host's real timeout. If the sweep returns\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"remainingMayExist: true"},{"type":"text","value":", another scheduled tick, queue message, or manual\nfollow-up can keep draining work."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"schema-setup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schema setup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime sweeps assume the durable store schema already exists. Host adapters do\nnot create tables during a cron tick, scheduled function, or worker alarm. That\nkeeps production behavior explicit: migrations and bootstrap steps belong to the\napp deploy/admin process, not the background sweep path."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For the Drizzle/Postgres adapter, Workflow owns the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workflow_*"},{"type":"text","value":" schema. Apply\nthe package-owned migration against the same database your deployed functions\nuse:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"psql \"$DATABASE_URL\" -f node_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql","lang":"bash"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For Cloudflare D1, use the D1-compatible package-owned migration artifact:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"node_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-cloudflare-d1/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql","lang":"txt"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wire it into your app scripts if your deploy provider runs package scripts:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"{\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"workflow:migrate\": \"psql \\\"$DATABASE_URL\\\" -f node_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql\",\n    \"workflow:sweep\": \"tsx scripts/workflow-sweep.ts\"\n  }\n}","lang":"json"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not copy Workflow's internal table declarations into your app Drizzle schema.\nKeep app code focused on workflow definitions and host entrypoints. Keep\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"store.ensureSchema()"},{"type":"text","value":" for tests, local demos, and explicit admin bootstrap\nscripts."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The migration creates "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workflow_schema_migrations"},{"type":"text","value":" so future store schema changes\ncan be tracked as package-owned numbered migrations. Apply new store migrations\nbefore rolling out a store adapter version that expects them."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"cloudflare","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare can run the same runtime shape with a Worker "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scheduled()"},{"type":"text","value":" handler:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createCloudflareWorkflowScheduledHandler } from '@tanstack/workflow-cloudflare'\n\nexport default {\n  scheduled: createCloudflareWorkflowScheduledHandler({\n    runtime: ({ env }) => createWorkflowRuntime(env),\n    maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n    maxTimers: 25,\n    maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n  }),\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare-specific store adapters are separate work. The current deployment\nPOC proves the host shape with Workers and Durable Objects."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"cloudflare-notes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare notes"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cron Triggers wake due work through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scheduled()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable Objects, D1, Queues, and Workers are natural adapter targets."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep each scheduled invocation bounded and let the store decide what is due."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"railway","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway Cron Jobs run a service on a crontab expression. The service should do\nbounded sweep work and then exit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create a small sweep command:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// scripts/workflow-sweep.ts\nimport { createRailwayWorkflowCronCommand } from '@tanstack/workflow-railway'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '../src/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nconst sweep = createRailwayWorkflowCronCommand({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  logSummary: true,\n})\n\nawait sweep()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure the Railway service with config-as-code:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"# railway.toml\n[deploy]\nstartCommand = \"pnpm workflow:sweep\"\ncronSchedule = \"*/5 * * * *\"\nrestartPolicyType = \"NEVER\"","lang":"toml"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Or with JSON:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://railway.com/railway.schema.json\",\n  \"deploy\": {\n    \"startCommand\": \"pnpm workflow:sweep\",\n    \"cronSchedule\": \"*/5 * * * *\",\n    \"restartPolicyType\": \"NEVER\"\n  }\n}","lang":"json"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the same durable store as the rest of your app."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"railway-notes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway notes"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cron Jobs run the service start command on a schedule and expect the process\nto terminate when the task is done."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway supports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"railway.toml"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"railway.json"},{"type":"text","value":" config-as-code for deploy\nsettings, including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startCommand"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cronSchedule"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway skips a cron run if the previous one is still running, so the store\nstill needs leases and idempotency for correctness."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway cron schedules use UTC and cannot run more frequently than every five\nminutes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway Postgres is a natural first durable store for app-embedded workflows."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"netlify","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Netlify"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"pnpm add @tanstack/workflow-netlify","lang":"bash"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create a Scheduled Function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// netlify/functions/workflow-sweep-background.ts\nimport {\n  createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler,\n} from '@tanstack/workflow-netlify'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '../../src/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nexport default createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n})\n\nexport const config = {\n  schedule: '*/5 * * * *',\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter returns a compact summary:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"{\n  \"ok\": true,\n  \"summary\": {\n    \"materialized\": 1,\n    \"scheduled\": { \"completed\": 1 },\n    \"timers\": {},\n    \"eventCount\": 8,\n    \"returnedEventCount\": 0\n  },\n  \"deadlineReached\": false,\n  \"remainingMayExist\": false\n}","lang":"json"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includeSweepResult: true"},{"type":"text","value":" only for debugging because it can return large\nevent arrays."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"netlify-notes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Netlify notes"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scheduled Functions are wake-up ticks."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Published deploys own scheduled function execution."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deploy Previews do not auto-tick scheduled functions. Use Netlify's manual\n\"Run now\" action to test the path before publishing."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a durable external store such as Postgres, Netlify Database, Neon, or\nanother store adapter."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply the store adapter's package-owned migration during deploy/setup. The\nscheduled function should only own host config and the sweep handler."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxDurationMs"},{"type":"text","value":" below the function timeout."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"vercel","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"pnpm add @tanstack/workflow-vercel","lang":"bash"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create a route handler:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// app/api/workflow/sweep/route.ts\nimport { createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler } from '@tanstack/workflow-vercel'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '@/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nexport const runtime = 'nodejs'\nexport const maxDuration = 60\n\nexport const GET = createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  cronSecret: process.env.CRON_SECRET,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"vercel.json"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://openapi.vercel.sh/vercel.json\",\n  \"crons\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"/api/workflow/sweep\",\n      \"schedule\": \"*/5 * * * *\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","lang":"json"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CRON_SECRET"},{"type":"text","value":" is configured, Vercel sends:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>","lang":"http"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter validates that header when you pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cronSecret"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"vercel-notes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel notes"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel Cron invokes the route with HTTP "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cron should wake the runtime, not hold workflow state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel Hobby projects are limited to daily cron cadence. For minute-level\nsweeps, use a plan that supports the cadence or call the sweep through another\nscheduler."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a durable external store such as Postgres. Function memory is not a store."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply the store adapter's package-owned migration during deploy/setup. The\nroute should only own host config and the sweep handler."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choosing-a-sweep-cadence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choosing a sweep cadence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sweep cadence is a product decision:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Need"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suggested cadence"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Daily digest or report"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Daily or hourly"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"User-visible delayed actions"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every minute"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Near-real-time timers"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queue plus frequent sweeps"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rare long sleeps"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Coarse cron plus manual/event wake-ups"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The cadence controls how quickly due work is noticed. The store still controls\nwhether work is due and whether a worker can claim it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"queue-integration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queue integration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queues are optional. They are useful for:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fanning out heavy work after a sweep finds many due items"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"retrying webhook delivery"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"waking another sweep when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"remainingMayExist"},{"type":"text","value":" is true"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"distributing starts and signals across workers"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"They are not the durability boundary. A queue message should point at a run,\ntimer, schedule bucket, or signal. The store remains the source of truth."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"failure-model","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Failure model"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assume:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"cron can deliver late"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"cron can deliver twice"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a function can crash after a side effect"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a worker can time out before releasing a lease"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a webhook can retry"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a deploy can happen while runs are paused"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The runtime/store model handles this with leases, idempotency keys, stable\nworkflow versions, and replay. Your application still needs idempotent external\nside effects."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"deployment-checklist","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deployment checklist"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure a durable store and run schema migrations."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep workflow loaders available for old versions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add one sweep entrypoint per deployment target."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set host timeout and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxDurationMs"},{"type":"text","value":" together."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep sweep event arrays disabled by default."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Log "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"summary"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deadlineReached"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"remainingMayExist"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alert on repeated stale lease recovery, sweep errors, and growing due timers."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Smoke test start, sleep, timer sweep, signal delivery, and attach/read flows."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"deployment","text":"Deployment","level":1},{"id":"invocation-shapes","text":"Invocation shapes","level":2},{"id":"bounded-sweeps","text":"Bounded sweeps","level":2},{"id":"schema-setup","text":"Schema setup","level":2},{"id":"cloudflare","text":"Cloudflare","level":2},{"id":"cloudflare-notes","text":"Cloudflare notes","level":3},{"id":"railway","text":"Railway","level":2},{"id":"railway-notes","text":"Railway notes","level":3},{"id":"netlify","text":"Netlify","level":2},{"id":"netlify-notes","text":"Netlify notes","level":3},{"id":"vercel","text":"Vercel","level":2},{"id":"vercel-notes","text":"Vercel notes","level":3},{"id":"choosing-a-sweep-cadence","text":"Choosing a sweep cadence","level":2},{"id":"queue-integration","text":"Queue integration","level":2},{"id":"failure-model","text":"Failure model","level":2},{"id":"deployment-checklist","text":"Deployment checklist","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n# Deployment\n\nTanStack Workflow is designed for normal deployment targets. A workflow can span\nhours or months, but each host invocation should do bounded work and then return.\n\nThe host is not the durability boundary. Cloudflare Cron Triggers, Railway Cron\nJobs, Netlify Scheduled Functions, Vercel Cron, queues, and worker alarms only\nwake the runtime. The store decides what is due, who can claim it, and whether\nthe run has already advanced.\n\nTanStack Workflow documentation and adapter work prioritizes partner\nenvironments first: Cloudflare, Railway, and Netlify. Vercel is still supported\nas an important compatibility target, but it should not be treated as the default\ndeployment path.\n\nThe deployment recipe is the same everywhere:\n\n1. Put workflow state in a durable store.\n2. Start or resume a run from HTTP, queue, webhook, or application code.\n3. Use host cron, alarms, or scheduled functions to wake `runtime.sweep()`.\n4. Keep every sweep under the host's timeout and memory budget.\n\n## Invocation shapes\n\nMost apps need three entrypoints:\n\n| Entrypoint | Runtime call | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Start a run | `runtime.startRun` | User action, API request, queue message |\n| Resume a run | `runtime.deliverSignal` or `runtime.deliverApproval` | Webhook, payment event, admin approval |\n| Wake background work | `runtime.sweep` | Cron, scheduled function, worker alarm |\n\nEach entrypoint claims a run, drives it to completion or the next pause, and\nreturns.\n\n## Bounded sweeps\n\nAlways budget background sweeps:\n\n```ts\nawait runtime.sweep({\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})\n```\n\nSet `maxDurationMs` below the host's real timeout. If the sweep returns\n`remainingMayExist: true`, another scheduled tick, queue message, or manual\nfollow-up can keep draining work.\n\n## Schema setup\n\nRuntime sweeps assume the durable store schema already exists. Host adapters do\nnot create tables during a cron tick, scheduled function, or worker alarm. That\nkeeps production behavior explicit: migrations and bootstrap steps belong to the\napp deploy/admin process, not the background sweep path.\n\nFor the Drizzle/Postgres adapter, Workflow owns the `workflow_*` schema. Apply\nthe package-owned migration against the same database your deployed functions\nuse:\n\n```bash\npsql \"$DATABASE_URL\" -f node_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql\n```\n\nFor Cloudflare D1, use the D1-compatible package-owned migration artifact:\n\n```txt\nnode_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-cloudflare-d1/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql\n```\n\nWire it into your app scripts if your deploy provider runs package scripts:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"workflow:migrate\": \"psql \\\"$DATABASE_URL\\\" -f node_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql\",\n    \"workflow:sweep\": \"tsx scripts/workflow-sweep.ts\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nDo not copy Workflow's internal table declarations into your app Drizzle schema.\nKeep app code focused on workflow definitions and host entrypoints. Keep\n`store.ensureSchema()` for tests, local demos, and explicit admin bootstrap\nscripts.\n\nThe migration creates `workflow_schema_migrations` so future store schema changes\ncan be tracked as package-owned numbered migrations. Apply new store migrations\nbefore rolling out a store adapter version that expects them.\n\n## Cloudflare\n\nCloudflare can run the same runtime shape with a Worker `scheduled()` handler:\n\n```ts\nimport { createCloudflareWorkflowScheduledHandler } from '@tanstack/workflow-cloudflare'\n\nexport default {\n  scheduled: createCloudflareWorkflowScheduledHandler({\n    runtime: ({ env }) => createWorkflowRuntime(env),\n    maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n    maxTimers: 25,\n    maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n  }),\n}\n```\n\nCloudflare-specific store adapters are separate work. The current deployment\nPOC proves the host shape with Workers and Durable Objects.\n\n### Cloudflare notes\n\n- Cron Triggers wake due work through `scheduled()`.\n- Durable Objects, D1, Queues, and Workers are natural adapter targets.\n- Keep each scheduled invocation bounded and let the store decide what is due.\n\n## Railway\n\nRailway Cron Jobs run a service on a crontab expression. The service should do\nbounded sweep work and then exit.\n\nCreate a small sweep command:\n\n```ts\n// scripts/workflow-sweep.ts\nimport { createRailwayWorkflowCronCommand } from '@tanstack/workflow-railway'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '../src/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nconst sweep = createRailwayWorkflowCronCommand({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  logSummary: true,\n})\n\nawait sweep()\n```\n\nConfigure the Railway service with config-as-code:\n\n```toml\n# railway.toml\n[deploy]\nstartCommand = \"pnpm workflow:sweep\"\ncronSchedule = \"*/5 * * * *\"\nrestartPolicyType = \"NEVER\"\n```\n\nOr with JSON:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://railway.com/railway.schema.json\",\n  \"deploy\": {\n    \"startCommand\": \"pnpm workflow:sweep\",\n    \"cronSchedule\": \"*/5 * * * *\",\n    \"restartPolicyType\": \"NEVER\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nUse the same durable store as the rest of your app.\n\n### Railway notes\n\n- Cron Jobs run the service start command on a schedule and expect the process\n  to terminate when the task is done.\n- Railway supports `railway.toml` and `railway.json` config-as-code for deploy\n  settings, including `startCommand` and `cronSchedule`.\n- Railway skips a cron run if the previous one is still running, so the store\n  still needs leases and idempotency for correctness.\n- Railway cron schedules use UTC and cannot run more frequently than every five\n  minutes.\n- Railway Postgres is a natural first durable store for app-embedded workflows.\n\n## Netlify\n\nInstall:\n\n```bash\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-netlify\n```\n\nCreate a Scheduled Function:\n\n```ts\n// netlify/functions/workflow-sweep-background.ts\nimport {\n  createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler,\n} from '@tanstack/workflow-netlify'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '../../src/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nexport default createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n})\n\nexport const config = {\n  schedule: '*/5 * * * *',\n}\n```\n\nThe adapter returns a compact summary:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"ok\": true,\n  \"summary\": {\n    \"materialized\": 1,\n    \"scheduled\": { \"completed\": 1 },\n    \"timers\": {},\n    \"eventCount\": 8,\n    \"returnedEventCount\": 0\n  },\n  \"deadlineReached\": false,\n  \"remainingMayExist\": false\n}\n```\n\nUse `includeSweepResult: true` only for debugging because it can return large\nevent arrays.\n\n### Netlify notes\n\n- Scheduled Functions are wake-up ticks.\n- Published deploys own scheduled function execution.\n- Deploy Previews do not auto-tick scheduled functions. Use Netlify's manual\n  \"Run now\" action to test the path before publishing.\n- Use a durable external store such as Postgres, Netlify Database, Neon, or\n  another store adapter.\n- Apply the store adapter's package-owned migration during deploy/setup. The\n  scheduled function should only own host config and the sweep handler.\n- Keep `maxDurationMs` below the function timeout.\n\n## Vercel\n\nInstall:\n\n```bash\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-vercel\n```\n\nCreate a route handler:\n\n```ts\n// app/api/workflow/sweep/route.ts\nimport { createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler } from '@tanstack/workflow-vercel'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '@/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nexport const runtime = 'nodejs'\nexport const maxDuration = 60\n\nexport const GET = createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  cronSecret: process.env.CRON_SECRET,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n})\n```\n\nConfigure `vercel.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://openapi.vercel.sh/vercel.json\",\n  \"crons\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"/api/workflow/sweep\",\n      \"schedule\": \"*/5 * * * *\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nWhen `CRON_SECRET` is configured, Vercel sends:\n\n```http\nAuthorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>\n```\n\nThe adapter validates that header when you pass `cronSecret`.\n\n### Vercel notes\n\n- Vercel Cron invokes the route with HTTP `GET`.\n- Cron should wake the runtime, not hold workflow state.\n- Vercel Hobby projects are limited to daily cron cadence. For minute-level\n  sweeps, use a plan that supports the cadence or call the sweep through another\n  scheduler.\n- Use a durable external store such as Postgres. Function memory is not a store.\n- Apply the store adapter's package-owned migration during deploy/setup. The\n  route should only own host config and the sweep handler.\n\n## Choosing a sweep cadence\n\nSweep cadence is a product decision:\n\n| Need | Suggested cadence |\n| --- | --- |\n| Daily digest or report | Daily or hourly |\n| User-visible delayed actions | Every minute |\n| Near-real-time timers | Queue plus frequent sweeps |\n| Rare long sleeps | Coarse cron plus manual/event wake-ups |\n\nThe cadence controls how quickly due work is noticed. The store still controls\nwhether work is due and whether a worker can claim it.\n\n## Queue integration\n\nQueues are optional. They are useful for:\n\n- fanning out heavy work after a sweep finds many due items\n- retrying webhook delivery\n- waking another sweep when `remainingMayExist` is true\n- distributing starts and signals across workers\n\nThey are not the durability boundary. A queue message should point at a run,\ntimer, schedule bucket, or signal. The store remains the source of truth.\n\n## Failure model\n\nAssume:\n\n- cron can deliver late\n- cron can deliver twice\n- a function can crash after a side effect\n- a worker can time out before releasing a lease\n- a webhook can retry\n- a deploy can happen while runs are paused\n\nThe runtime/store model handles this with leases, idempotency keys, stable\nworkflow versions, and replay. Your application still needs idempotent external\nside effects.\n\n## Deployment checklist\n\n- Configure a durable store and run schema migrations.\n- Keep workflow loaders available for old versions.\n- Add one sweep entrypoint per deployment target.\n- Set host timeout and `maxDurationMs` together.\n- Keep sweep event arrays disabled by default.\n- Log `summary`, `deadlineReached`, and `remainingMayExist`.\n- Alert on repeated stale lease recovery, sweep errors, and growing due timers.\n- Smoke test start, sleep, timer sweep, signal delivery, and attach/read flows.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: deployment\ntitle: Deployment\n---\n\n# Deployment\n\nTanStack Workflow is designed for normal deployment targets. A workflow can span\nhours or months, but each host invocation should do bounded work and then return.\n\nThe host is not the durability boundary. Cloudflare Cron Triggers, Railway Cron\nJobs, Netlify Scheduled Functions, Vercel Cron, queues, and worker alarms only\nwake the runtime. The store decides what is due, who can claim it, and whether\nthe run has already advanced.\n\nTanStack Workflow documentation and adapter work prioritizes partner\nenvironments first: Cloudflare, Railway, and Netlify. Vercel is still supported\nas an important compatibility target, but it should not be treated as the default\ndeployment path.\n\nThe deployment recipe is the same everywhere:\n\n1. Put workflow state in a durable store.\n2. Start or resume a run from HTTP, queue, webhook, or application code.\n3. Use host cron, alarms, or scheduled functions to wake `runtime.sweep()`.\n4. Keep every sweep under the host's timeout and memory budget.\n\n## Invocation shapes\n\nMost apps need three entrypoints:\n\n| Entrypoint | Runtime call | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Start a run | `runtime.startRun` | User action, API request, queue message |\n| Resume a run | `runtime.deliverSignal` or `runtime.deliverApproval` | Webhook, payment event, admin approval |\n| Wake background work | `runtime.sweep` | Cron, scheduled function, worker alarm |\n\nEach entrypoint claims a run, drives it to completion or the next pause, and\nreturns.\n\n## Bounded sweeps\n\nAlways budget background sweeps:\n\n```ts\nawait runtime.sweep({\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})\n```\n\nSet `maxDurationMs` below the host's real timeout. If the sweep returns\n`remainingMayExist: true`, another scheduled tick, queue message, or manual\nfollow-up can keep draining work.\n\n## Schema setup\n\nRuntime sweeps assume the durable store schema already exists. Host adapters do\nnot create tables during a cron tick, scheduled function, or worker alarm. That\nkeeps production behavior explicit: migrations and bootstrap steps belong to the\napp deploy/admin process, not the background sweep path.\n\nFor the Drizzle/Postgres adapter, Workflow owns the `workflow_*` schema. Apply\nthe package-owned migration against the same database your deployed functions\nuse:\n\n```bash\npsql \"$DATABASE_URL\" -f node_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql\n```\n\nFor Cloudflare D1, use the D1-compatible package-owned migration artifact:\n\n```txt\nnode_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-cloudflare-d1/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql\n```\n\nWire it into your app scripts if your deploy provider runs package scripts:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"workflow:migrate\": \"psql \\\"$DATABASE_URL\\\" -f node_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql\",\n    \"workflow:sweep\": \"tsx scripts/workflow-sweep.ts\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nDo not copy Workflow's internal table declarations into your app Drizzle schema.\nKeep app code focused on workflow definitions and host entrypoints. Keep\n`store.ensureSchema()` for tests, local demos, and explicit admin bootstrap\nscripts.\n\nThe migration creates `workflow_schema_migrations` so future store schema changes\ncan be tracked as package-owned numbered migrations. Apply new store migrations\nbefore rolling out a store adapter version that expects them.\n\n## Cloudflare\n\nCloudflare can run the same runtime shape with a Worker `scheduled()` handler:\n\n```ts\nimport { createCloudflareWorkflowScheduledHandler } from '@tanstack/workflow-cloudflare'\n\nexport default {\n  scheduled: createCloudflareWorkflowScheduledHandler({\n    runtime: ({ env }) => createWorkflowRuntime(env),\n    maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n    maxTimers: 25,\n    maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n  }),\n}\n```\n\nCloudflare-specific store adapters are separate work. The current deployment\nPOC proves the host shape with Workers and Durable Objects.\n\n### Cloudflare notes\n\n- Cron Triggers wake due work through `scheduled()`.\n- Durable Objects, D1, Queues, and Workers are natural adapter targets.\n- Keep each scheduled invocation bounded and let the store decide what is due.\n\n## Railway\n\nRailway Cron Jobs run a service on a crontab expression. The service should do\nbounded sweep work and then exit.\n\nCreate a small sweep command:\n\n```ts\n// scripts/workflow-sweep.ts\nimport { createRailwayWorkflowCronCommand } from '@tanstack/workflow-railway'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '../src/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nconst sweep = createRailwayWorkflowCronCommand({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  logSummary: true,\n})\n\nawait sweep()\n```\n\nConfigure the Railway service with config-as-code:\n\n```toml\n# railway.toml\n[deploy]\nstartCommand = \"pnpm workflow:sweep\"\ncronSchedule = \"*/5 * * * *\"\nrestartPolicyType = \"NEVER\"\n```\n\nOr with JSON:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://railway.com/railway.schema.json\",\n  \"deploy\": {\n    \"startCommand\": \"pnpm workflow:sweep\",\n    \"cronSchedule\": \"*/5 * * * *\",\n    \"restartPolicyType\": \"NEVER\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nUse the same durable store as the rest of your app.\n\n### Railway notes\n\n- Cron Jobs run the service start command on a schedule and expect the process\n  to terminate when the task is done.\n- Railway supports `railway.toml` and `railway.json` config-as-code for deploy\n  settings, including `startCommand` and `cronSchedule`.\n- Railway skips a cron run if the previous one is still running, so the store\n  still needs leases and idempotency for correctness.\n- Railway cron schedules use UTC and cannot run more frequently than every five\n  minutes.\n- Railway Postgres is a natural first durable store for app-embedded workflows.\n\n## Netlify\n\nInstall:\n\n```bash\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-netlify\n```\n\nCreate a Scheduled Function:\n\n```ts\n// netlify/functions/workflow-sweep-background.ts\nimport {\n  createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler,\n} from '@tanstack/workflow-netlify'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '../../src/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nexport default createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n})\n\nexport const config = {\n  schedule: '*/5 * * * *',\n}\n```\n\nThe adapter returns a compact summary:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"ok\": true,\n  \"summary\": {\n    \"materialized\": 1,\n    \"scheduled\": { \"completed\": 1 },\n    \"timers\": {},\n    \"eventCount\": 8,\n    \"returnedEventCount\": 0\n  },\n  \"deadlineReached\": false,\n  \"remainingMayExist\": false\n}\n```\n\nUse `includeSweepResult: true` only for debugging because it can return large\nevent arrays.\n\n### Netlify notes\n\n- Scheduled Functions are wake-up ticks.\n- Published deploys own scheduled function execution.\n- Deploy Previews do not auto-tick scheduled functions. Use Netlify's manual\n  \"Run now\" action to test the path before publishing.\n- Use a durable external store such as Postgres, Netlify Database, Neon, or\n  another store adapter.\n- Apply the store adapter's package-owned migration during deploy/setup. The\n  scheduled function should only own host config and the sweep handler.\n- Keep `maxDurationMs` below the function timeout.\n\n## Vercel\n\nInstall:\n\n```bash\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-vercel\n```\n\nCreate a route handler:\n\n```ts\n// app/api/workflow/sweep/route.ts\nimport { createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler } from '@tanstack/workflow-vercel'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '@/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nexport const runtime = 'nodejs'\nexport const maxDuration = 60\n\nexport const GET = createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  cronSecret: process.env.CRON_SECRET,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n})\n```\n\nConfigure `vercel.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://openapi.vercel.sh/vercel.json\",\n  \"crons\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"/api/workflow/sweep\",\n      \"schedule\": \"*/5 * * * *\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nWhen `CRON_SECRET` is configured, Vercel sends:\n\n```http\nAuthorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>\n```\n\nThe adapter validates that header when you pass `cronSecret`.\n\n### Vercel notes\n\n- Vercel Cron invokes the route with HTTP `GET`.\n- Cron should wake the runtime, not hold workflow state.\n- Vercel Hobby projects are limited to daily cron cadence. For minute-level\n  sweeps, use a plan that supports the cadence or call the sweep through another\n  scheduler.\n- Use a durable external store such as Postgres. Function memory is not a store.\n- Apply the store adapter's package-owned migration during deploy/setup. The\n  route should only own host config and the sweep handler.\n\n## Choosing a sweep cadence\n\nSweep cadence is a product decision:\n\n| Need | Suggested cadence |\n| --- | --- |\n| Daily digest or report | Daily or hourly |\n| User-visible delayed actions | Every minute |\n| Near-real-time timers | Queue plus frequent sweeps |\n| Rare long sleeps | Coarse cron plus manual/event wake-ups |\n\nThe cadence controls how quickly due work is noticed. The store still controls\nwhether work is due and whether a worker can claim it.\n\n## Queue integration\n\nQueues are optional. They are useful for:\n\n- fanning out heavy work after a sweep finds many due items\n- retrying webhook delivery\n- waking another sweep when `remainingMayExist` is true\n- distributing starts and signals across workers\n\nThey are not the durability boundary. A queue message should point at a run,\ntimer, schedule bucket, or signal. The store remains the source of truth.\n\n## Failure model\n\nAssume:\n\n- cron can deliver late\n- cron can deliver twice\n- a function can crash after a side effect\n- a worker can time out before releasing a lease\n- a webhook can retry\n- a deploy can happen while runs are paused\n\nThe runtime/store model handles this with leases, idempotency keys, stable\nworkflow versions, and replay. Your application still needs idempotent external\nside effects.\n\n## Deployment checklist\n\n- Configure a durable store and run schema migrations.\n- Keep workflow loaders available for old versions.\n- Add one sweep entrypoint per deployment target.\n- Set host timeout and `maxDurationMs` together.\n- Keep sweep event arrays disabled by default.\n- Log `summary`, `deadlineReached`, and `remainingMayExist`.\n- Alert on repeated stale lease recovery, sweep errors, and growing due timers.\n- Smoke test start, sleep, timer sweep, signal delivery, and attach/read flows.\n"}
